1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,290
There are only four kinds of wrong
answers on SAT inference questions.

2
00:00:05,433 --> 00:00:06,433
Too strong.

3
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:08,432
Out of scope.

4
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:11,682
True in real life, but not in the passage.

5
00:00:12,111 --> 00:00:14,555
And the fourth one looks completely right,

6
00:00:14,575 --> 00:00:17,361
because it uses the passage's own words.

7
00:00:17,431 --> 00:00:18,982
The first three are quick to spot.

8
00:00:19,002 --> 00:00:21,982
The fourth is reversed
logic: the right words,

9
00:00:22,002 --> 00:00:23,268
with the relationship flipped.

10
00:00:23,288 --> 00:00:25,911
That's question six, the hardest one,

11
00:00:25,931 --> 00:00:27,037
so stay for it.

12
00:00:27,145 --> 00:00:30,090
The rule for all four:
pick what has to be true,

13
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:31,717
not what could be true.

14
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:34,252
Six SAT-style questions.

15
00:00:34,788 --> 00:00:37,217
Keep score, and see if
you can get all six.

16
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:40,692
An inference question gives you a
short passage that builds an argument.

17
00:00:40,942 --> 00:00:44,085
A few facts, a claim, maybe a contrast.

18
00:00:44,477 --> 00:00:46,763
Then the last sentence stops halfway.

19
00:00:46,906 --> 00:00:48,871
Your job is to finish the reasoning.

20
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:50,553
Think of it like the last line of a proof.

21
00:00:50,573 --> 00:00:54,160
The lines above it have to lead there.

22
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:57,087
The answer isn't a guess
about what else might be true,

23
00:00:57,107 --> 00:01:00,215
and it isn't something you happen
to know from outside the passage.

24
00:01:00,269 --> 00:01:03,698
These are Inference questions, part
of the Information and Ideas domain,

25
00:01:03,768 --> 00:01:06,412
which is about a quarter of the
Reading and Writing section.

26
00:01:07,447 --> 00:01:09,534
Reading and Writing
passages come from science,

27
00:01:09,554 --> 00:01:12,035
history and social
studies, the humanities,

28
00:01:12,055 --> 00:01:13,055
and literature.

29
00:01:13,094 --> 00:01:14,844
They're easy to recognize.

30
00:01:15,629 --> 00:01:17,681
The blank is at the
very end of the passage,

31
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:20,110
and the question is
almost always the same:

32
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:22,666
which choice most logically
completes the text?

33
00:01:22,712 --> 00:01:24,640
Now look at the words
right before the blank.

34
00:01:25,390 --> 00:01:27,462
They tell you what kind
of ending you need.

35
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:32,605
Therefore, so, or thus means a conclusion.

36
00:01:32,855 --> 00:01:35,604
However or but means a contrast.

37
00:01:36,283 --> 00:01:39,692
Suggests or most likely
means a careful conclusion,

38
00:01:39,712 --> 00:01:40,926
not a certain one.

39
00:01:40,989 --> 00:01:41,989
Here's the method.

40
00:01:42,631 --> 00:01:44,774
Step one: trace the logic.

41
00:01:45,203 --> 00:01:48,361
Mark each claim, and how it
connects to the next one.

42
00:01:48,381 --> 00:01:49,524
Is it a cause?

43
00:01:49,595 --> 00:01:50,846
A contrast?

44
00:01:50,917 --> 00:01:52,703
A condition, like only if?

45
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:54,942
Step two: predict.

46
00:01:55,406 --> 00:01:58,656
Before you look at the choices,
finish the sentence in your own words.

47
00:01:58,763 --> 00:02:00,314
A rough prediction is fine.

48
00:02:00,334 --> 00:02:02,192
It keeps the choices from steering you.

49
00:02:02,254 --> 00:02:05,824
Step three: choose the
answer the text guarantees.

50
00:02:06,146 --> 00:02:09,804
For each choice, ask: does
the passage force this?

51
00:02:09,824 --> 00:02:12,805
If it needs an extra fact, or a
bigger leap than the passage makes,

52
00:02:12,825 --> 00:02:13,603
it's out.

53
00:02:13,623 --> 00:02:14,623
Question one.

54
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:18,460
Tardigrades, tiny animals
often found in moss,

55
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,802
can survive being completely dried out.

56
00:02:20,979 --> 00:02:23,638
As they lose water, they
curl into a compact,

57
00:02:23,658 --> 00:02:27,730
dormant form called a tun, in which
their metabolism nearly stops.

58
00:02:28,195 --> 00:02:31,826
When water returns, a tun can
rehydrate and become active again.

59
00:02:31,846 --> 00:02:35,203
A tardigrade that looks lifeless
in a sample of dried moss,

60
00:02:35,632 --> 00:02:37,453
then, blank

61
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:38,597
Pause and try it.

62
00:02:41,815 --> 00:02:42,815
Trace it.

63
00:02:43,065 --> 00:02:45,280
Dried out tardigrades become tuns.

64
00:02:45,494 --> 00:02:48,244
Tuns can become active
again when water returns.

65
00:02:48,494 --> 00:02:51,957
So a tardigrade that looks dead
in dry moss might just be a tun.

66
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:54,672
Prediction: it could still be alive.

67
00:02:54,754 --> 00:02:58,663
Choice B: may still be alive, and could
become active again if the moss is

68
00:02:58,683 --> 00:02:59,683
moistened.

69
00:03:00,004 --> 00:03:01,076
That's the answer.

70
00:03:01,576 --> 00:03:03,076
Notice the word may.

71
00:03:03,469 --> 00:03:06,199
The passage says tardigrades
can survive drying out,

72
00:03:06,219 --> 00:03:07,611
not that every one does.

73
00:03:07,861 --> 00:03:10,897
So a careful answer is the right fit.

74
00:03:10,989 --> 00:03:14,274
A gets the logic backward: it
says the tardigrade has died.

75
00:03:14,632 --> 00:03:18,275
D is too strong:
certainly, within minutes.

76
00:03:18,917 --> 00:03:22,024
And C is the interesting one.

77
00:03:22,131 --> 00:03:25,560
Tardigrades really have survived
exposure to outer space.

78
00:03:26,096 --> 00:03:29,025
But the passage never mentions space.

79
00:03:29,775 --> 00:03:32,953
True in real life is not the
same as supported by the text.

80
00:03:33,035 --> 00:03:34,086
Question two.

81
00:03:34,106 --> 00:03:37,622
The Venus flytrap, a plant native to
wetlands in North and South Carolina,

82
00:03:37,642 --> 00:03:40,106
catches insects with
leaves that snap shut.

83
00:03:40,356 --> 00:03:44,085
Each leaf has several trigger hairs,
and a leaf closes only if its hairs are

84
00:03:44,105 --> 00:03:47,052
touched at least twice
within about twenty seconds.

85
00:03:47,072 --> 00:03:51,322
An insect crawling across a leaf usually
brushes the hairs more than once,

86
00:03:51,572 --> 00:03:56,108
but a raindrop or a falling seed
typically touches a hair just once.

87
00:03:56,858 --> 00:04:00,608
This requirement therefore
most likely blank

88
00:04:00,686 --> 00:04:01,793
Pause and try it.

89
00:04:05,011 --> 00:04:06,011
Trace it.

90
00:04:06,225 --> 00:04:11,440
Insects usually touch
the hairs more than once.

91
00:04:12,011 --> 00:04:13,670
Raindrops and seeds typically touch once.

92
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:14,634
A leaf needs two touches.

93
00:04:14,654 --> 00:04:17,367
So the rule filters out most of
the things that aren't insects.

94
00:04:18,082 --> 00:04:20,940
Prediction: fewer false alarms.

95
00:04:21,015 --> 00:04:26,066
Choice D: it reduces how often the plant
closes a leaf on objects that are not

96
00:04:26,086 --> 00:04:27,031
prey.

97
00:04:27,051 --> 00:04:28,086
Reduces.

98
00:04:28,264 --> 00:04:30,065
That's exactly as strong as the passage,

99
00:04:30,085 --> 00:04:31,479
and no stronger.

100
00:04:31,693 --> 00:04:33,085
D is the answer.

101
00:04:33,144 --> 00:04:37,160
Now compare A: it prevents the plant from
ever closing on anything other than a

102
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:38,465
living insect.

103
00:04:38,787 --> 00:04:39,731
Ever?

104
00:04:39,751 --> 00:04:43,251
The passage says raindrops and
seeds typically touch a hair once.

105
00:04:43,857 --> 00:04:45,410
Typically isn't always.

106
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:46,929
That's the too strong trap.

107
00:04:46,974 --> 00:04:53,026
Words like ever, always, never, all,
and proves need a passage that's just as

108
00:04:53,046 --> 00:04:53,987
absolute.

109
00:04:54,007 --> 00:04:55,579
B and C are out of scope.

110
00:04:55,756 --> 00:04:58,936
The passage says nothing about
how fast the leaves close,

111
00:04:59,007 --> 00:05:01,114
or how many insects live in the wetlands.

112
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:02,175
Question three.

113
00:05:02,711 --> 00:05:07,496
When the price of a good rises,
people generally buy less of it.

114
00:05:08,817 --> 00:05:13,261
Economists call demand for a good
inelastic when the percentage drop in the

115
00:05:13,281 --> 00:05:16,675
quantity people buy is smaller
than the percentage rise in price.

116
00:05:16,736 --> 00:05:18,915
Studies have found that in the short run,

117
00:05:19,129 --> 00:05:22,700
demand for gasoline in the
United States is inelastic,

118
00:05:23,058 --> 00:05:27,538
in part because many drivers can't
quickly change how far they need to drive.

119
00:05:27,558 --> 00:05:31,164
If gasoline prices rose by
ten percent over a few weeks,

120
00:05:31,343 --> 00:05:36,040
economists would therefore expect the
quantity of gasoline people buy to blank

121
00:05:36,093 --> 00:05:37,200
Pause and try it.

122
00:05:41,418 --> 00:05:42,363
Trace it.

123
00:05:42,383 --> 00:05:44,668
Price goes up, so people buy less.

124
00:05:44,775 --> 00:05:45,899
That's a drop.

125
00:05:45,918 --> 00:05:50,561
Inelastic means the percent drop is
smaller than the percent rise in price.

126
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:53,133
Gas is inelastic in the short run.

127
00:05:53,347 --> 00:05:54,990
Prices rise ten percent.

128
00:05:55,061 --> 00:05:58,061
So the quantity falls, but
by less than ten percent.

129
00:05:58,490 --> 00:05:59,490
That's choice C.

130
00:05:59,682 --> 00:06:02,325
A says the quantity falls
by more than ten percent.

131
00:06:02,468 --> 00:06:05,289
That's the definition of
inelastic turned upside down.

132
00:06:05,754 --> 00:06:07,697
This is the reversed logic trap.

133
00:06:07,717 --> 00:06:11,384
It uses the right words, but the
relationship runs the wrong way.

134
00:06:11,404 --> 00:06:14,134
B goes too far: stay exactly the same.

135
00:06:14,154 --> 00:06:17,313
Can't quickly change isn't the
same as can't change at all,

136
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:20,118
and the passage says
people generally buy less.

137
00:06:20,369 --> 00:06:22,563
And nothing in the passage supports D,

138
00:06:22,583 --> 00:06:23,869
the quantity going up.

139
00:06:23,937 --> 00:06:25,009
Question four.

140
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:31,345
In the eighteen eighties, the French
painters Georges Seurat and Paul Signac

141
00:06:31,365 --> 00:06:34,346
developed pointillism, a
technique in which small,

142
00:06:34,366 --> 00:06:37,274
distinct dots of color
are applied to a canvas.

143
00:06:37,294 --> 00:06:39,631
Rather than mixing every
shade on a palette,

144
00:06:39,651 --> 00:06:41,794
pointillists relied on the viewer's eye:

145
00:06:41,937 --> 00:06:45,703
from a distance, neighboring dots of
different colors can appear to blend into a

146
00:06:45,723 --> 00:06:46,901
single tone.

147
00:06:46,923 --> 00:06:50,081
This effect, however, depends
on the dots being too small,

148
00:06:50,101 --> 00:06:53,566
from where the viewer stands,
to be seen as separate marks.

149
00:06:54,279 --> 00:06:58,153
A viewer standing just a few inches from
a pointillist painting would therefore

150
00:06:58,173 --> 00:07:00,468
most likely blank

151
00:07:00,525 --> 00:07:01,633
Pause and try it.

152
00:07:05,851 --> 00:07:06,851
Trace it.

153
00:07:06,994 --> 00:07:10,494
The blending only happens when the dots
are too small to see as separate marks.

154
00:07:10,922 --> 00:07:13,295
A few inches away, they
aren't too small anymore.

155
00:07:13,315 --> 00:07:15,028
So the blending breaks down.

156
00:07:15,315 --> 00:07:17,116
Prediction: you'd see the separate dots.

157
00:07:17,136 --> 00:07:18,351
That's choice A.

158
00:07:18,384 --> 00:07:21,684
B reverses it: the colors
blend even more up close.

159
00:07:21,704 --> 00:07:23,098
C is out of scope.

160
00:07:23,491 --> 00:07:25,791
It compares pointillism
with Impressionist painting,

161
00:07:25,811 --> 00:07:27,455
which the passage never mentions.

162
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:30,383
It sounds like art history,
so it feels relevant.

163
00:07:30,669 --> 00:07:33,241
But the passage gives you
nothing to judge it with.

164
00:07:33,384 --> 00:07:35,027
That's the out of scope trap.

165
00:07:35,115 --> 00:07:38,559
And D, unable to tell what
the painting shows at all,

166
00:07:38,579 --> 00:07:39,973
is too strong.

167
00:07:40,473 --> 00:07:43,686
Seeing separate dots doesn't mean
you can't recognize anything.

168
00:07:43,759 --> 00:07:45,152
So those are the four traps.

169
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:49,417
Too strong: the answer claims
more than the passage can prove.

170
00:07:49,437 --> 00:07:53,079
Out of scope: it's about something
the passage never discusses.

171
00:07:53,258 --> 00:07:55,615
Reversed: it flips the relationship.

172
00:07:55,722 --> 00:07:58,239
And true in real life: it might be a fact,

173
00:07:58,259 --> 00:07:59,759
but it isn't in the text.

174
00:07:59,816 --> 00:08:02,618
On harder questions, the
logic takes an extra step,

175
00:08:02,638 --> 00:08:04,423
and the traps get harder to see.

176
00:08:04,816 --> 00:08:05,706
Here are two.

177
00:08:05,726 --> 00:08:07,119
Question five.

178
00:08:07,761 --> 00:08:11,992
Dendrochronologists, scientists
who date wood using tree rings,

179
00:08:12,012 --> 00:08:16,349
match a sample's pattern of wide and
narrow rings to a master record built from

180
00:08:16,369 --> 00:08:20,440
trees of known age, which lets them
assign a calendar year to each ring.

181
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:22,714
If a beam still has its outermost ring,

182
00:08:22,734 --> 00:08:25,627
the last one the tree grew
before it was cut down,

183
00:08:26,234 --> 00:08:28,663
researchers can tell
when the tree was felled.

184
00:08:29,091 --> 00:08:31,321
But a beam's outer
rings are often missing,

185
00:08:31,341 --> 00:08:33,241
trimmed away when the beam was shaped.

186
00:08:33,261 --> 00:08:37,098
So if a trimmed beam's outermost
surviving ring dates to twelve fifty,

187
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:41,038
researchers can conclude only
that the tree was cut down blank

188
00:08:41,071 --> 00:08:42,178
Pause and try it.

189
00:08:46,397 --> 00:08:47,611
Trace it carefully.

190
00:08:49,039 --> 00:08:53,147
The passage tells you the outermost
ring is the last one the tree grew.

191
00:08:53,361 --> 00:08:55,504
So the outer rings are the newest.

192
00:08:55,824 --> 00:08:59,217
On this beam, some outer
rings were trimmed off.

193
00:08:59,504 --> 00:09:01,968
Those missing rings
grew after twelve fifty.

194
00:09:02,006 --> 00:09:04,220
So the tree was still
alive in twelve fifty,

195
00:09:04,505 --> 00:09:07,399
and it may have kept growing
for years after that.

196
00:09:07,649 --> 00:09:09,935
It was cut down in twelve
fifty at the earliest.

197
00:09:10,685 --> 00:09:12,665
Prediction: no earlier than twelve fifty.

198
00:09:12,685 --> 00:09:13,792
That's choice C.

199
00:09:13,836 --> 00:09:17,193
B is the reversed trap: no
later than twelve fifty.

200
00:09:17,693 --> 00:09:19,621
Same number, logic flipped.

201
00:09:19,836 --> 00:09:21,228
A is too strong.

202
00:09:21,263 --> 00:09:26,086
Exactly twelve fifty would only work
if the outermost ring were still there.

203
00:09:26,335 --> 00:09:30,851
And D sounds reasonable, but the passage
says nothing about when beams were

204
00:09:30,871 --> 00:09:31,779
installed.

205
00:09:31,799 --> 00:09:32,799
Last one.

206
00:09:33,157 --> 00:09:34,335
The hardest.

207
00:09:34,442 --> 00:09:38,514
Ice cores drilled in Antarctica
contain tiny bubbles of ancient air.

208
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:41,136
Each year's snow buries
the snow beneath it,

209
00:09:41,156 --> 00:09:43,717
slowly compressing older layers into ice.

210
00:09:43,737 --> 00:09:47,538
Near the surface, though, air still
moves between the grains of packed snow,

211
00:09:47,558 --> 00:09:51,288
and pockets of air aren't sealed off
from the atmosphere until they're roughly

212
00:09:51,308 --> 00:09:53,645
forty to one hundred twenty meters deep.

213
00:09:53,665 --> 00:09:57,951
As a result, the air
trapped in a bubble is blank

214
00:09:57,981 --> 00:09:59,124
Pause and try it.

215
00:10:00,196 --> 00:10:01,874
Take a little longer on this one.

216
00:10:06,073 --> 00:10:07,073
Trace it.

217
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:10,073
Each year's snow buries the snow below it.

218
00:10:10,287 --> 00:10:13,374
So snow that's forty to one hundred
twenty meters down has been buried for a

219
00:10:13,394 --> 00:10:14,159
long time.

220
00:10:14,179 --> 00:10:15,231
It's old.

221
00:10:15,251 --> 00:10:18,374
But the air between its grains kept
mixing with the atmosphere the whole time.

222
00:10:18,394 --> 00:10:20,529
The air only gets locked
in at the very end.

223
00:10:20,549 --> 00:10:24,601
So when a bubble finally seals,
the ice around it is old,

224
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:26,835
and the air inside it is newer.

225
00:10:27,228 --> 00:10:29,799
The air is younger than
the ice that surrounds it.

226
00:10:30,157 --> 00:10:31,799
The answer is D.

227
00:10:31,835 --> 00:10:34,871
A is the reversed trap:
older than the ice.

228
00:10:35,585 --> 00:10:39,692
It sounds right, because the
passage calls it ancient air.

229
00:10:40,406 --> 00:10:42,763
But trace the steps, and it's backward.

230
00:10:42,844 --> 00:10:44,609
And C is true in real life.

231
00:10:44,629 --> 00:10:48,895
Scientists really do use the air in
ice cores to study past carbon dioxide

232
00:10:48,915 --> 00:10:49,915
levels.

233
00:10:50,486 --> 00:10:52,665
But the blank starts with as a result.

234
00:10:53,129 --> 00:10:56,216
The answer has to follow
from that sealing delay,

235
00:10:56,236 --> 00:10:57,558
and C doesn't.

236
00:10:58,308 --> 00:11:01,772
The words before the blank tell
you what job the answer has to do.

237
00:11:01,798 --> 00:11:02,743
Here's your test-day routine.

238
00:11:02,763 --> 00:11:07,584
Read the passage once, tracing how
each sentence connects to the next.

239
00:11:07,834 --> 00:11:10,334
Then look hard at the words
right before the blank.

240
00:11:10,691 --> 00:11:14,671
They tell you the job: a
conclusion, a contrast,

241
00:11:14,691 --> 00:11:16,012
or a careful maybe.

242
00:11:16,079 --> 00:11:18,544
Predict the ending before
you look at the choices.

243
00:11:18,901 --> 00:11:21,416
Then test each choice with one question:

244
00:11:21,436 --> 00:11:23,222
does the text force this?

245
00:11:24,151 --> 00:11:26,952
Cross out anything too
strong, out of scope,

246
00:11:26,972 --> 00:11:29,400
reversed, or true only in real life.

247
00:11:29,467 --> 00:11:31,217
Match the strength of the passage.

248
00:11:31,466 --> 00:11:36,018
If it says may, often, or typically,
the right answer will usually be just as

249
00:11:36,038 --> 00:11:37,073
careful.

250
00:11:37,146 --> 00:11:40,181
An absolute word in a
choice is a warning sign.

251
00:11:40,275 --> 00:11:44,953
On average, you have a little over a
minute per Reading and Writing question.

252
00:11:45,346 --> 00:11:48,612
These passages can be dense, so
don't reread the whole thing.

253
00:11:48,632 --> 00:11:51,453
Reread the sentence
that sets up the blank.

254
00:11:51,510 --> 00:11:52,546
Recap.

255
00:11:52,582 --> 00:11:56,847
Trace the logic, predict the ending, and
choose the answer the text guarantees.

256
00:11:56,867 --> 00:11:58,596
Not the one that could be true.

257
00:11:58,616 --> 00:12:00,046
The one that has to be.

258
00:12:00,096 --> 00:12:02,861
Every question in this video
was written for this video,

259
00:12:02,881 --> 00:12:04,524
in the style of the real test.

260
00:12:05,167 --> 00:12:08,861
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

261
00:12:08,881 --> 00:12:10,810
skill, including Inferences.

262
00:12:10,865 --> 00:12:12,972
If this helped, subscribe.

263
00:12:13,079 --> 00:12:18,238
Next up: Words in Context, and how to
get those questions right even when you

264
00:12:18,258 --> 00:12:19,401
don't know the word.
